+++ b/domains/findings/pages/a-page-value-in-a-property-sidecar-is-judged-as-a-constant.finding.ts
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+import type { Finding } from "../finding.page-type.ts"
+
+export const aPageValueInAPropertySidecarIsJudgedAsAConstant = {
+  id: "01a07772-7a74-7851-b18a-8ed4110869fa",
+  pageTypeSlug: "finding",
+  slug: "a-page-value-in-a-property-sidecar-is-judged-as-a-constant",
+  domainSlug: "domain/akasha-check",
+  claim:
+    "`identifier-matches-its-place` reads the value a property sidecar holds as a constant and requires `upper-snake-case`, so a `.uncommitted.ts` or `.declaration.ts` file is refused for the very name its own stem makes. `pageValueIn` skips a page's own value, but it answers nothing where the file name carries sections, and a sidecar always carries one. 10 tracked files are refused this way, and the uncommitted sidecars make up most of the 117 between the 392 refusals over tracked files and the 509 an audit reports. These files are machine-written, so the refusal falls on whatever next writes one rather than on an author who chose the name.",
+  evidence:
+    "Measured 2026-09-06 over every `.ts` and `.tsx` git tracks, and against `akasha audit --check identifier-matches-its-place` for the untracked ones.\n\n`pageValueIn` at `identifier-matches-its-place.code-check.code.ts:187` calls `partedIn(at)` and answers null where `said.sections.length > 0`. A sidecar's name always carries a section, so the page's own value is never recognised and falls through to `constantsIn` at line 193, which takes any top-level `const` bound to an object literal and holds it to `constant-identifier`.\n\nThe 10 tracked ones are the `workflows` value in `changes/workflow-templates/pages/*/*.workflow-template.declaration.ts`.\n\nThe untracked ones are `.uncommitted.ts` sidecars: 8 under `agents/claude-accounts/pages/`, 8 under `errors-core/runtime-errors/pages/`, and the `alan/tracking/to-dos/pages/` set. Each is refused for a name like `aawaltonClaudeAccountUncommitted` or `breakfastToDoUncommitted`.\n\nThe check's own departure invariant says the name skipped is the one the file's stem makes, and a sidecar's export name is exactly that: `aawalton.claude-account.uncommitted.ts` exports `aawaltonClaudeAccountUncommitted`. `page-file-name.module.ts` says a file whose only section is `uncommitted` holds the uncommitted values of that file's page, and is never a property's file nor a page. So the value is a page's rather than a constant, and the stem rule already covers it. What fails is that `pageValueIn` reads a section as proof the file holds no page value.\n\nThe same reading would skip a `.sops.ts` file, which `page-file-name` treats the same way, though none is refused today.",
+} as const satisfies Finding
